.TH __EVENT_LOOP_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__event_loop_destroy \- internal destroy function
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

void __event_loop_destroy(event_loop loop);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
loop is an opaque VALUE handle (a packed {slot index, generation}
pair), resolved through a library-owned slot table before the
underlying reactor object is ever touched. loop must be a
currently live handle (one returned by event_loop_create/
_with_mprocs and not yet destroyed). EVENT_LOOP_INVALID is a
silent no-op. Any other value (a stale handle already destroyed by
an earlier call or concurrently by another thread, a forged value,
or garbage) is a FATAL ERROR: this function calls fatal_err()
(abort()/SIGABRT) rather than risk a use-after-free or double-free.

On a live handle, waits for every in-flight resolved use of the
handle to finish (event_loop_add/_modify/_pause/_resume/_remove/
_reg_count calls currently executing on another thread), then
calls event_loop_shutdown() if the caller skipped it (guaranteeing
every reactor thread is joined and no dispatch can be in flight
before the next step), then frees every remaining registration
through the same per-registration path event_loop_remove() uses.

For queue-backed registrations this correctly unlinks each one from
its queue's own waiter list before freeing it, so a queue that
outlives this event_loop is never left with a dangling waiter
pointer. fd registrations are not closed and queues are not
destroyed; only the event_loop's own registration bookkeeping is
freed.

Do not call directly; use the event_loop_destroy macro, which also
sets the handle to EVENT_LOOP_INVALID.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ___event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR event_loop_shutdown (3),
.BR event_loop_remove (3)
